Here is a breakdown of your code:

1. You first check if the two input strings have the same length. If they don't, you immediately return `false`, because anagrams are strings with the same characters in the same quantity, but possibly in a different order, so strings of different lengths cannot be anagrams.

2. If the strings have the same length, you sort both strings and compare them. If they are equal after sorting, that means they are anagrams, so you return `true`. If they are not equal, that means they are not anagrams, so you return `false`.

This approach works, but it has a time complexity of O(n log n) because of the sorting. If you're dealing with large strings, this could be slow. In such cases, you could use a different approach, like counting the frequency of each character in the strings. This would give you a time complexity of O(n), which is much faster.

Here's how you could do it:

```c
bool isAnagram(string str1, string str2) {
    if (str1.length() != str2.length()) {
        return false;
    }

    int count[256] = {0}; // assuming ASCII characters only

    for (char c : str1) {
        count[c]++;
    }

    for (char c : str2) {
        count[c]--;
    }

    for (int i = 0; i < 256; i++) {
        if (count[i] != 0) {
            return false;
        }
    }

    return true;
}
```

